# Standup — the AIR's instructions (removed when done)

You are the AIR of this new workspace. Your identity is the workspace's, not
this folder's. The Operator is the human who made the folders and ran the
script; they answer your questions. The checker is the **Standup Conductor**
on the conducting seat, and it speaks in the **Workspace Standup** group. You
execute; the Standup Conductor certifies. You never certify yourself.

Three standing rules for this whole file:

- Never open, read or print `nostr.key`, `agent.env` or `agent.local.env`.
  Point tools at them by path and let the tool read them.
- Look first: nothing overwrites a key already in an agent's home. A key the
  Operator placed there is used as it stands.
- The Operator's token is asked for once and lives only in git's credential
  store. This file names the repository and the ask, never the token.

Every path below is relative to the workspace root — the folder you were
launched from is `nethub/nexus/`, so `cd` back up to the root first.

Work the thirteen steps in order. Each one ends with its check. Do not move
on until the check passes.

1. **The token and the owner name.** Ask the Operator for a fine-grained
   GitHub token, read-only on the `standup-guy` repository, and for their
   GitHub owner name (the account or organisation that holds it). Set the
   credential store first — `git config --global credential.helper osxkeychain`
   (or `store` where the keychain helper is unavailable) — so git keeps the
   token and you never hold it. Never echo the token, never write it to a
   file, never put it in a URL you print. Check: the Operator has given you
   an owner name and git has a credential helper configured.

2. **Standup Guy, once, into his permanent home.** Clone him straight into
   the home he keeps forever:
   `git clone https://github.com/<owner>/standup-guy nethub/standup-guy`
   (git will ask for the token; the helper stores it). Nothing of him is
   cloned anywhere else, moved or deleted afterwards: that clone is his home
   in this workspace, and his updates arrive later by `git pull` in it.
   Check: `nethub/standup-guy/CLAUDE.md` exists.

3. **Buzz.app, installed and verified.** Source: the releases of
   `github.com/block/buzz` — `Buzz_<version>_aarch64.dmg` on Apple Silicon,
   `Buzz_<version>_x64.dmg` on Intel — or the Homebrew cask,
   `brew install --cask block-buzz`, which installs the same app. Verify the
   installed copy before trusting it: `codesign --verify --deep --strict
   /Applications/Buzz.app` and `spctl --assess --type execute
   /Applications/Buzz.app` must both pass; the app is notarized under
   Developer ID team `EYF346PHUG` with bundle id `xyz.block.buzz.app`. The
   bundled binaries you need — `buzz`, `buzz-acp`, `buzz-agent` and
   `git-credential-nostr` — live in `/Applications/Buzz.app/Contents/MacOS/`.
   Details, including why there is no checksum file, are in
   `buzz-app-install.md` beside this template in Standup Guy's `standup/`
   folder. Check: both verification commands pass and the four binaries are
   present.

4. **nethub, fetched around him.** `nethub/` is not empty, so never clone
   into it — init in place and fetch. The relay serves repositories at
   `<relay>/git/<owner pubkey>/<repo>`, and the credential helper is the one
   Buzz.app ships. From the workspace root:

```
cd nethub
git init
git config credential.helper /Applications/Buzz.app/Contents/MacOS/git-credential-nostr
git config nostr.keyfile "$PWD/standup-guy/nostr.key"
git remote add origin https://blockbuzzmain-production-28a2.up.railway.app/git/acb8de002a0fb8fa6c978de87bdec78f2a9d943973d49534e51bbb8a13500c55/nethub
git fetch origin
git checkout -b main origin/main
```

   The `nostr.keyfile` line points git at Standup Guy's key by path; you do
   not open it. The checkout refuses if a tracked path already exists on
   disk — that refusal is the safety, and it does not fire here because
   nethub ignores `standup-guy/`, `nexus/` and `roadwork/`. Check:
   `nethub/CLAUDE.md` exists.

5. **The group, and your announcement under his key.** Standup Guy's
   identity is already a member of the **Workspace Standup** group; you
   speak there under his key until you have your own. Run `buzz` the way his
   own scripts do — in a subshell that sources his environment file, so the
   shell reads it and you never print it:
   `sh -c 'set -a; . nethub/standup-guy/agent.env; set +a; buzz channels list'`
   shows the group's id; post with `buzz messages send --channel <id>` the
   same way. Announce this workspace's standup; the post's last line is
   exactly:

   `posted by the AIR of the <X> Workspace under Standup Guy's key during standup`

   with `<X>` replaced by this workspace's name. From here on the checker
   answers you in that group. Check: your announcement is visible in the
   group.

6. **The seed, copied in as a plain object.** Fetch the nexus seed from the
   relay the same way as step 4, into a temporary folder; copy its tree —
   without `.git` — into `nethub/nexus/`; delete the temporary folder. The
   object is not a repository; it has no remote and no history. Check:
   `nethub/nexus/CLAUDE.md` exists and holds a statement-of-authority
   placeholder.

7. **The work plans.** `git clone https://github.com/2ndcortex-labs/roadwork
   nethub/roadwork` — a standup step, not a later command; the repository is
   public, so no key is needed. Make it pull-only: set its push URL to a
   refusing value so nothing here ever updates it. Check:
   `nethub/roadwork/ordering.md` exists.

8. **The connector's key, in the connector's own home.** Look first. Run this
   block inside `nethub/nexus/cchbas/ccbra/`:

```
# run inside nethub/nexus/cchbas/ccbra/ — look first; nothing overwrites a key already here
if [ -f nostr.key ]; then echo "key present - kept"; else (umask 077 && openssl rand -hex 32 > nostr.key) && echo "key made"; fi
```

   Then derive `pub.key` exactly as the connector construct's `README.md`
   says. Every other agent's key is made the same way, in that agent's own
   home, with the same look-first block; a key the Operator placed there is
   used as it stands. The checker verifies this by look-first — it reads
   whether a key exists, never the key. Check: each agent home holds its own
   `nostr.key` and `pub.key`, and no pre-existing key changed.

9. **Supervision.** Render the connector construct's four launchd templates,
   `nethub/nexus/cchbas/ccbra/launchd/*.plist.template`, into
   `nethub/nexus/cchbas/ccbra/launchd-rendered/` — the construct's `README.md`
   gives every placeholder — and load them. Check: `launchctl print
   gui/$(id -u)/<label>` shows each job running.

10. **The commons.** Confirm it is readable through Standup Guy: after his
    daily check, or an out-of-cycle refresh you ask him for,
    `nethub/reference/repos/` holds `repo-sops` and
    `buzz-agentic-architecture`. Check: both folders are present and
    readable.

11. **The statement, the charter, the beacon.** Fill the statement of
    authority in `nethub/nexus/CLAUDE.md` with the repositories this
    workspace holds the seat over. Fill `intent/intent.md` and
    `plan/plan.md` — their placeholders say the instance is not stood up
    while they remain. Then generate the root beacon: take the primitive's
    template (Appendix C of `context/workspace-reorg.md` in the info store,
    read from the commons at
    `nethub/reference/repos/buzz-agentic-architecture/`) onto the workspace
    root's `CLAUDE.md`, a position you first verify is empty of
    hand-authored content. Check: none of the three placeholders
    remains and the root beacon stands.

12. **Report to the checker.** Tell the **Standup Conductor**, gate by gate,
    in the **Workspace Standup** group. Give it your connector's `pub.key`
    value; the conductor has Repo Man add your connector to the group. From
    your first post under your own connector key — your announcement — you
    speak as yourself, no longer under Standup Guy's key. Done means all
    seven:

    (a) you act on the relay under your own connector key — your first post
        under it is your announcement;
    (b) the connector survives a reboot — launchd relaunches it;
    (c) the commons is readable through Standup Guy;
    (d) the work plans are present;
    (e) the statement of authority is filled and `intent/intent.md` and
        `plan/plan.md` are not placeholders;
    (f) the root beacon stands;
    (g) `CLAUDE.local.md` is gone.

13. **Remove this file.** Last, after the conductor certifies (a) through
    (f): `rm nethub/nexus/CLAUDE.local.md`. Do not remove it earlier — you
    need it until the last gate is certified. Its removal is (g), and (g) is
    the conductor's last check.

## A second owner

a person who is not the conducting seat's Operator is admitted to the Workspace Standup group by that Operator, is certified by the same checker, and holds their workspace after

Nothing here names any seat, so the same text serves every standup, whoever
the owner is and whichever seat conducts.
